_OMITTED_MARKER\n    remaining = max_chars - len(marker)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return message[:max_chars]\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nExample budget 32, marker 15 → remaining 17, head 9, tail 8. ✓ matches spec.\n\nDoes environ.py already have the new config? Evidence shows yes: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(lambda v: _int_or_none_min(v, 32))` and `_int_or_none_min` defined. Good — but check \"错误文字须包含变量名\" — EnvVar wraps exception with name. Good. Also empty string \"\" → int(\"\") raises ValueError with message; wrapped includes name. Good.\n\nHmm but `EnvVar(lambda v: ...)` — the descriptor created without going through factory; name set via __set_name__. Fine.\n\nBut is `_int_or_none_min` actually present in the file? Evidence ev-a736... shows it at lines 18-40. Yes.\n\nSo environ.py done. logger.py apparently NOT done despite replace_text — maybe the replace_text was applied to something else. Let me just check and apply.\n\nI need concrete old_text strings for logger.py edits, which I must know. I can do programmatic replacement with known substrings from the original source (shown in evidence). Use replace_text with old_text that I construct via variables from read content? Simpler: build new content in Python using string splits on known anchors.\n\nApproach for logger.py: use content c and do targeted replacements with `.replace(old, new, 1)` and assert counts, then write_file with full new content.\n\nAnchors from original:\n1. `        high_precision: bool = False,\\n        use_color: bool = False,\\n    ) -> None:\\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\\n        self.use_color = use_color\\n`\n   → replace with version including `whitespace`... new:\n```\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n        *,\n        max_messag